\p{How can a model describe many possible fields and average what we
observe? A probability law on a finite set of configurations assigns
probability weights to those fields and averages their observables. The
[16 binary fields on the four-cycle](fdip-0029) give a concrete
example: summing weights over configurations with the same retained
values produces the retained law. These probability weights are chosen
independently of the edge energies. Real probability weights on binary
fields are distinct from real-valued field coordinates. Observable
algebras and normalized functionals express this operation without assuming a finite
configuration set.}

\p{A supplied algebra map pulls a functional back by precomposition.
Over the reals or complexes, positivity and the involution determine
which functionals are states; the [real dual-number example](fdip-0025)
explains why the hypotheses matter. Coordinate restriction then pulls
polynomial observables back. For real fields with infinitely many
configurations, [reference measure](fdip-003U) and
[Gaussian integrability](fdip-0017) give the corresponding expectations.}

